##### 3.4.1.1 Like-Sign Technique

The same event like-sign approach is adopted to subtract the background from the same-event unlike-sign (\(\pi^{+}\pi^{-}\)) pair invariant mass spectrum. The advantage of like-sign technique is the like-sign pairs are taken from the same event so that there is no event structure difference between the like-sign spectrum and the unlike-sign spectrum. There is no bias due to the multiplicity and the \(z\)-vertex of the event.

In this measurement, the real pair represents all the possible combinations of \(\pi^{+}\pi^{-}\) invariant mass distributions in an event and the total background is calculated from the geometric mean of the invariant mass distribution obtained from

